'''''Polemon christyi''''', also known [[Common name|commonly]] as '''Christy's snake-eater''' and the '''eastern snake-eater''', is a [[species]] of mildly [[venomous]] [[Opisthoglyphous|rear-fanged]] [[snake]] in the [[subfamily]] [[Aparallactinae]] of the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Atractaspididae]]. The species is native to [[Central Africa]] and [[East Africa]].<ref name=RDB>{{NRDB species|genus=Polemon|species=christyi|accessdate=25 February 2019}}</ref><ref name="Portillo et al. 2019"/>

==Etymology==
The [[Specific name (zoology)|specific name]], ''christyi'', is in honor of English physician [[Cuthbert Christy]], who presented the [[Holotype|type specimen]] to the [[British Museum (Natural History)]].<ref name=Blgr1903>[[George Albert Boulenger|Boulenger GA]] (1903). "Descriptions of new Snakes in the Collection of the British Museum". ''Annals and Magazine of Natural History, Seventh Series'' '''12''': 350–354. (''Miodon christyi'', new species, p. 354).</ref><ref name=EDR>[[species:Bo Beolens|Beolens, Bo]]; [[species:Michael Watkins|Watkins, Michael]]; [[species:Michael Grayson|Grayson, Michael]] (2011). ''The Eponym Dictionary of Reptiles''. Baltimore: Johns Hopkins University Press. xiii + 296 pp. {{ISBN|978-1-4214-0135-5}}. (''Polemon christyi'', p. 54; ''P. leopoldi'', p. 155).</ref> The synonym, "''leopoldi'' ", is in honor of [[Leopold III of Belgium|Leopold III, King of the Belgians]].<ref name=EDR/>

==Geographic range==
Distribution of ''Polemon christyi'' include [[Uganda]], the [[Democratic Republic of the Congo]],  [[South Sudan]], and western [[Kenya]]. The status of observations from [[Rwanda]] and [[Malawi]] is uncertain, whereas observations from [[Tanzania]], [[Zambia]], and possibly [[Burundi]] likely refer to ''[[Polemon ater]]'' described in 2019.<ref name="Portillo et al. 2019"/>

==Habitat==
The preferred natural [[habitat]]s of ''P. christyi'' are [[forest]] and [[savanna]], at altitudes of {{convert|600|–|1,700|m|ft|abbr=on}}.<ref name=iucn/>

==Description==
The [[Dorsum (anatomy)|dorsum]] of the body of ''P. christyi'' is black. The ventral surface of the head is also black. The [[Ventral scales|ventrals]] and [[Subcaudal scales|subcaudals]] are white, broadly margined with black.

The [[Type (biology)|type specimen]], a female, is {{convert|43|cm|in|frac=8|abbr=on}} in total length, which includes a tail {{convert|28|mm|in|frac=8|abbr=on}} long.

The [[dorsal scales]] are smooth, without apical pits, and are arranged in 15 rows at midbody. The ventrals number 209. The [[Anal scale|anal plate]] is divided. The subcaudals number 20, also divided.

The diameter of the eye is three fifths its distance from the mouth. The [[Rostral scale|rostral]] is higher than wide, barely visible from above. The [[Internasal scales|internasals]] are slightly shorter than the [[Prefrontal scales|prefrontals]]. The [[Frontal scale|frontal]] is slightly longer than broad, much broader than the [[Supraocular scales|supraoculars]], as long as its distance from the rostral, much shorter than the [[Parietal scales|parietals]]. The [[Nasal scale|nasal]] is entire, in contact with the [[Ocular scales|preocular]]. There are two postoculars. The [[Temporal scales|temporals]] are 1+1. There are seven [[Supralabial scale|upper labials]], the third and fourth entering the eye. There are four [[Sublabial scale|lower labials]] in contact with the anterior [[Chin shields|chin shield]], the first lower labial forming a suture with its fellow behind the [[Mental scale|mental]]. There are two pairs of chin shields, the anterior pair larger than the posterior pair.<ref name=Blgr1903/>

==Diet==
''P. christyi'' [[Predation|preys]] upon snakes, including those of its own species.<ref name=iucn/>

==Reproduction==
''P. christyi'' is [[Oviparity|oviparous]].<ref name=RDB/>

